WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.380
Minions… or monsters?

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:06.660
The new Amazon series “The Rings of Power”
portrays Orcs in a much different light than

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.410
the iconic film trilogy.

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:12.660
Keep watching to find out how faithful to
the original text they really are.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.700
When you sit down to watch a live-action version
of a J.R.R.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:21.160
Tolkien story, it's virtually guaranteed that
you'll see folks like Hobbits, Elves and Dwarves

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.650
over the course of the adventure.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:26.619
Likewise, at one point or another, there's
going to be Orcs.

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:30.900
These twisted, brutal creatures oppose the
protagonists of "The Lord of the Rings'' on

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:36.663
multiple occasions, and make up the bulk of
Sauron's and Saruman's armies in climactic battles.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:42.601
Peter Jackson's "The Hobbit" film trilogy
even features prominent Orc-lords Azog and Bolg.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:47.050
The Orcs in the various movies are imposing
and fearsome-looking, and they hiss, grunt

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:49.120
and roar exactly like you'd assume.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.579
However, in the grand scheme of things, the
live-action orc groups we've seen so far are

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:58.570
essentially just factions that happen to oppose
the heroes because they serve the villain,

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:01.249
and it's their literal job description to
do so.

00:01:01.249 --> 00:01:05.820
Sure, as Boromir fatally finds out in "The
Lord of the Rings: Fellowship of the Ring,"

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.370
particularly strong Orcs like the Uruk-Hai
have been known to achieve major victories.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:15.350
Still, by and large, orcs serve the same purpose
as Stormtroopers do in "Star Wars" movies

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.480
— they're unsympathetic cannon fodder for
the heroes to mow down by the dozen.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:25.669
That is, until Amazon Prime Video's critically-acclaimed
"The Lord of the Rings: The Rings of Power" turned up.

00:01:25.689 --> 00:01:30.300
The series doesn't take long to establish
Orcs as a true terror, and in doing so, the

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.540
show finally makes them as horrifying as Tolkien
intended.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:38.430
Every Tolkien-themed live-action project has
managed to portray Orcs as creepy creatures.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:44.040
However, "The Rings of Power" Episode 2 outright
turns them into horror movie villains by leaning

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:49.590
hard on their digging abilities, which Tolkien
has noted rival the Dwarves themselves.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:54.689
These Orcs don't just look like slasher movie
monsters, either — they also play the part.

00:01:54.689 --> 00:01:56.580
They provide copious jump scares.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.710
They stalk terrified, hiding people.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:04.119
And as Bronwyn and Theo find out firsthand,
they're extremely hard to kill when you're

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:07.909
not a powerful hero armed with rare and luxurious
weaponry.

00:02:07.909 --> 00:02:12.290
Even well-equipped and trained Elven warrior
Arondir gets the scare of his lifetime as

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:17.239
he flees the terrifying enemies ... and then,
just when he least expects it, several clawed

00:02:17.239 --> 00:02:21.090
hands emerge from the darkness and grab him
from behind.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.890
It's horrifying stuff, and a very good take
on Tolkien's Orcs.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:28.290
Out of all the different beings in "The Lord
of the Rings," they might just be the most

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:32.800
twisted, but they're still more or less cannon
fodder in the movies — which is far from

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.280
the unyielding monsters Tolkien wrote them
as.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.959
The author wrote about Orcs and their creator
— Sauron's old boss Melkor, aka Morgoth

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:41.640
— in "The Fall of Gondolin":

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:47.110
"Their hearts were of granite and their bodies
deformed; foul their faces which smiled not,

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:51.680
but their laugh that of the clash of metal,
and to nothing were they more fain than to

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.390
aid in the basest of the purposes of Melko."

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:57.360
Now, which portrayal fits that description
better?

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:04.047
The unruly minions of the movies, or the ruthless,
stalking beasts in "The Rings of Power" Episode 2?

00:03:04.069 --> 00:03:08.790
According to an IGN article about "The Rings
of Power's" Orc game, the feral and terrifying

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:13.300
version of Orcs we see in the show is a very
premeditated approach.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.620
Executive producer Lindsey Weber said,

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:20.250
"We spent a lot of time talking about what
it would mean to be an Orc in the Second Age.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:24.690
It felt appropriate that their look would
be different, part of a wilder, more raw,

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:28.360
Second Age Middle-earth, closer to where the
First Age ends.

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:32.439
As we meet them, they're not yet organized
into armies, they're a little more scattered

00:03:32.439 --> 00:03:34.049
and they've been scavenging."

00:03:34.049 --> 00:03:40.042
Weber also teased that the show will feature
all sorts of different Orcs, including female ones.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:43.610
Interestingly, the unnerving effect of the
show's Orcs is so strong that even the actors

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:45.100
were thrown off by them.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:50.260
As Lloyd Owen (who plays notable Númenórean
Elendil in the show) told Metro, the Orcs

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:52.569
legitimately creeped him out on set.

00:03:52.569 --> 00:03:53.569
The actor said,

00:03:53.569 --> 00:03:55.700
"You get a visceral reaction to it.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:57.079
Which is rejection ultimately.

00:03:57.079 --> 00:03:59.150
'I don't want to be around that.'"

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:03.390
Based on what we've seen so far, it's difficult
to imagine anyone wanting to be around the

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:07.630
show's Orcs for any amount of time ... but
from the looks of it, the people of Middle-earth

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:09.180
might not have any other option.

